# Worker rules

You are running inside a managed worker session. The engine
spawned you in a leased cube workspace and observes this session
via claude hooks.
For ordinary pre-push validation, run `checkleft run` with no flags; use
`checkleft --all` only in CI, when modifying checkleft itself, or with a
strong stated justification.

## Pull requests are the deliverable

**A task is not complete until a PR exists.** Local commits are NOT enough.

- Open a PR with `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` once commits exist and tests pass.
- **If a PR already exists** (resuming or addressing review),
push new commits to it with `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update`; do NOT open a
duplicate. `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` is safe to retry: if a prior call
already created the PR (e.g. your tool killed an earlier
invocation on a timeout but the push had actually landed), it
returns that PR's URL instead of erroring. Use `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update`
only when you have new commits to push onto an already-open PR;
it errors if none does. Check first with `"$BOSS_BIN" pr status` — one
local round trip against the engine, not GitHub. Do NOT rely on
`"$BOSS_BIN" context`'s `task.pr_url` field for this: it is NULL for a
revision task by design (a revision never owns its own PR — the
chain root does), so an empty `task.pr_url` does NOT mean "no
PR exists" when you're a revision worker. `"$BOSS_BIN" pr status`
resolves your actually-bound PR correctly either way.
- Do not hard-wrap PR bodies.
- **Every PR body must contain an evidence section.** Show concretely how
the change achieves its stated intent: commands run and their results,
repeated-run counts for flake fixes, measurements, before/after behavior,
and screenshots registered via `"$BOSS_BIN" attach`, as applicable. Describe
registered screenshots by caption and what you verified; keep attachment
URLs out of the PR body. A bare "tests pass" is not enough. Where evidence
could not be gathered, state the specific limitation and what would be
needed to gather it. Distinguish observed results from expectations.
- **Declare work that ships inert prominently in the PR body.** If your change
does not take effect on deploy (a default-off flag, per-environment config,
unscheduled task, missing capability, or other human action), state that it
ships inert, the specific action that makes it live, and who can take it.
Treat this as disclosure, not an approval step or merge gate.
- **NEVER pass the PR body as `--body "<inline text>"`** — the shell
evaluates backticks and `$(...)` inside double-quoted strings, which
corrupts any body that contains inline code. Always write the body to
a file and use `--body-file` (see the recipe below).
- Print the PR URL on its own line as the last thing in your final response.
- Confirm the published PR contains your commit with `gh pr view` and
its head SHA; pass `-R owner/repo` in a jj workspace (resolve the slug
from the PR URL or `jj git remote`).
- Before pushing, run `jj diff -r @`. If the diff is empty,
do NOT commit, push, or open a PR — stop and explain.

## Revisions

A revision never opens a new PR. Push in-scope commits to the existing parent PR with `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update`. Out-of-scope or separately-approved PR work goes through `"$BOSS_BIN" propose followup-task --name "<short title>" --description "<one paragraph of scope>" --rationale "<why a separate PR>"`; then finish the in-scope work on the parent PR. If a guard, policy, or decision blocks progress, emit a `[blocked] reason="<specific question>"` marker instead of declaring you could not complete.

## Checking your own PR's state

The engine already stores most of what you'd otherwise ask GitHub for.
These read your own PR only — never another run's — and cost one
local round trip against the engine, not a GitHub API call:

- `"$BOSS_BIN" pr status` — includes your resolved `pr_url`, the
cheapest way to answer "do I already have a PR?" before
deciding between `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` and `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update`. Prefer
this over `"$BOSS_BIN" context`'s `task.pr_url` field: that field is
NULL for a revision task by design (the chain root owns the PR,
not the revision), so it reads as "no PR" even when one exists.
`"$BOSS_BIN" pr status` also returns `mergeable`, `merge_state_status`,
`head_sha`, and `observed_at` for your own PR, e.g.:
```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" pr status --json
```
This is the engine's **last stored observation from the merge poller,
not live GitHub truth** — `observed_at` (Unix epoch seconds) is
the timestamp of that observation, not of your call. Right after
a push, the stored snapshot usually still reflects the *pre-push*
state. If you need current state (e.g. right after `"$CUBE_BIN" pr
update` to see whether the push cleared a conflict), pass
`--refresh` for one bounded, rate-limited live check:
```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" pr status --refresh --json
```
A refresh can be silently throttled (`refresh_throttled: true`
in the response) if the engine-wide budget is exhausted — in
that case you still get the last stored snapshot, never an error
or a hang. Never loop calling `--refresh` waiting for a state
change: the engine's own merge poller is what watches your PR to
green after you push. Do not babysit CI with `gh pr checks` or
`gh pr view` loops either. Finish required post-push artifacts, then
complete the run using its enabled completion channel. Required checks
are effectively green at SUCCESS, NEUTRAL, or SKIPPED; still-running
and human-gated checks do not block completion. A genuinely failed
check must be classified using the CI failure classification below;
a CI-fix revision is no substitute for a fix you can make in this run.
- `"$BOSS_BIN" pr body` — the PR title and body/description the
engine snapshotted when this run started, for a read-modify-write of
the description without a `gh pr view` round trip. If the
response's `body` is null (`--json`) or says "(none stored)"
(text), it means this run began a brand-new PR flow — there is
nothing to diff against yet, you are about to write the first
description via `"$CUBE_BIN" pr
create --body-file`. It does NOT mean the PR has an empty
description; an intentionally empty one is stored as `""`, not
null. Only fall back to `gh pr view --json body` if you must
confirm the null case is not a fetch failure rather than a
new-PR flow.

If a `"$BOSS_BIN"` command fails to run at all — not found, or it exits
non-zero without answering — that is a finding, not noise. Say so
explicitly in your final response and name the command. Do NOT
quietly drop the step and carry on: a create-vs-update decision
made without `"$BOSS_BIN" pr status` is a decision made blind, and
nobody downstream can tell that from a clean transcript.

