## Always use absolute paths — never `cd`

Your shell already starts in your workspace, so a `cd` buys you nothing
and costs you the run: the agent harness refuses to auto-approve a
compound command that pairs a `cd` with a relative file read (and, for
`grep`/`rg`/`diff`/`git`/`cp`/`mv`, refuses outright), so the session
stops on a permission dialog with no human watching it.

- Do NOT write `cd <dir> && <command>`, and do not `cd` as a separate
step either.
- Address every file and directory by its absolute path:
`cat /abs/path/to/file.rs`, `rg pattern /abs/path/to/dir`,
`sed -n '1,40p' /abs/path/to/file.rs`.
- For a tool that must run from a directory, use its own flag rather
than a `cd` — e.g. `git -C <abs dir> …`, `make -C <abs dir> …`.

This is about the command shape, not about what you may access: the
engine's guards resolve every path the same way however it is spelled,
so writing paths in full is never a way around a boundary.

## VCS

Use `jj` for all VCS. Do not invoke `git` directly except via `gh`.

- `jj git fetch` to sync; `jj new main@origin` for a fresh task;
`jj edit <bookmark>` to resume.
- `jj describe -m '...'` to set commit messages;
`jj bookmark set <name> -r @` to create or advance a bookmark.
- **NEVER push branches or open PRs with bare VCS commands** (`jj git push`,
`git push`, `gh pr create`). A PreToolUse hook blocks these. Use:
- `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create --branch <name>` — new PR (pushes branch + opens PR, jj-aware, no GIT_DIR needed)
- `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update --branch <name>` — existing PR (pushes new commits to it)
- Never `jj git push --deleted` or `git push --delete`
without explicit user approval.
- **Never run raw `git` against cube's shared source store** — the
checkout your workspace's `.jj/repo` pointer names, under cube's repos
root — whether by `cd`-ing into it or by pointing `GIT_DIR`, `--git-dir`
or `-C` at it. A PreToolUse hook refuses every git write there and every
shallow `fetch`/`pull`/`clone` (`--depth`, `--shallow-since`, …) that
would touch it: one such fetch turns the shared store into a shallow
clone and breaks every dispatch for that repository. Read-only git
against the store (`log`, `show`, `diff`, `rev-parse`, …) is allowed.
To reproduce a CI script that runs raw git, clone into a throwaway
directory under your own temp dir and run it there.
- `.claude/` is gitignored by the engine. Do not force-track
or commit anything inside it (no `--force`,
no `jj file track .claude/...`).

### Commit messages must be inline

Always pass `-m "…"` to `git commit`, `git rebase`, `jj commit`,
`jj describe`, and amend/squash flows (`git commit --amend`,
`jj squash`, `jj split`). The worker environment has no usable
`$EDITOR` — commands that fall through to one fail. Fix by
re-running with `-m`.

## Creating a PR from a jj workspace

Cube workspaces are secondary jj workspaces. There is no `.git/`
at the workspace root, so bare `gh` calls fail with
`fatal: not a git repository`. Use `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` instead —
it resolves the remote `owner/repo` from `jj git remote` and
passes `-R <owner/repo>` to `gh`, so no `GIT_DIR` guess is needed.

### Canonical PR creation recipe

Write the PR body to a temp file — never embed it inline on the command
line. This protects backticks, `$(...)`, and `${VAR}` from shell evaluation.

```sh
jj describe -m "your commit message"
jj bookmark set my-feature -r @
body=$(mktemp)
cat > "$body" << 'PRBODY'
## Summary
Your description here. Inline code like `crate-name` and `$(cmd)` is safe.
PRBODY
"$CUBE_BIN" pr create --branch my-feature --title "Your PR title" --body-file "$body"
```

`"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` is safe to retry: if an open PR already exists for
the branch, it returns that PR's URL instead of erroring, without
pushing again. Use `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update` only when you have new commits
to push onto an already-open PR (see below). `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` handles
the push and `--allow-new` automatically; never call `jj git push` directly.

To update an existing PR (push new commits to it):

```sh
"$CUBE_BIN" pr update --branch my-feature   # pushes to the PR; errors if none exists
```

### `origin` is the real GitHub upstream (shared object store)

Every cube workspace is a **secondary jj workspace** that SHARES one
object store with its siblings — there is no per-workspace clone. That
store has a single `origin` remote pointing at the real GitHub upstream.

- **Only use `"$CUBE_BIN" pr create` / `"$CUBE_BIN" pr update` for pushes and PR operations**:
they push to GitHub by URL and resolve `-R <owner/repo>` for `gh`
automatically, so PR creation Just Works without `.git/` at the root.
Direct `jj git push`, `git push`, or `gh pr create` calls are blocked
by a PreToolUse hook in every worker session.
- Because the store is shared, a `jj git fetch` in ANY workspace
advances the remote-tracking bookmarks (e.g. `main@origin`) seen by
ALL of them. Don't be alarmed if refs move without you fetching.
- A solid belt-and-suspenders check that a push actually landed is to
compare your local commit against GitHub's head sha (do not infer
success from the push command's own output alone):

```sh
# local commit you intended to ship
jj log -r my-feature --no-graph -T commit_id
# what GitHub actually has (must match)
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/branches/my-feature --jq .commit.sha
# for a specific PR head:
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> --jq .head.sha
```

## Merge-conflict telemetry

If you ever run `"$CUBE_BIN" workspace rebase` mid-task (e.g. to sync with
`main` before pushing, or because a push was rejected) and it reports
`REBASED_WITH_CONFLICTS`, resolve the conflicts as normal, then
AFTER resolving run:

```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" engine conflicts record-producer --execution-id <your execution id> \
--head-branch <branch> --base-branch <main_branch> --files <comma-separated conflicted paths>
```

using the `branch`, `main_branch`, and conflicted-file list `cube
workspace rebase` printed (or `jj resolve --list` if you need it
again). This is telemetry only — best-effort, never blocks or
reverts your actual work — but it is the only way the engine learns
about conflicts you resolve on your own, before they'd ever reach
the reviewed in-PR conflict-resolution flow.

## Reuse before you build

Before implementing any cross-cutting capability (an API/HTTP client,
config loading, caching, logging wiring, retry/backoff, or similar
infrastructure), search the repo for an existing implementation
first. If one exists, reuse or extend it instead of writing a new
one. If duplication is genuinely necessary, say so explicitly in the
PR description with the reason — a justified, visible
exception is the only way to avoid a "revision required"
reuse/duplication finding from the automated reviewer.

## Boundaries

- Do not modify files outside this workspace. Sibling workspaces
belong to other workers.
- Do not modify cube's database, lease state, or workspace registry.
- `~/Library/Application Support/Boss/` is engine-only.
Never read, write, or touch it. Do not query the database
yourself; ask for any task or product context you need.
`bossctl` is not available to workers.

## Capturing a screenshot for your own verification

**Do not commit capture PNGs** — and do not delete them either.
Attach them:

```sh
"$BOSS_BIN" attach /tmp/shot.png --caption "wide table, after the fix"
```

The engine stores the image outside this workspace (which is
recycled after your run), for your own verification and for
someone looking at this run locally. `"$BOSS_BIN" attach --list` shows
what this task already has, across runs.

Validation is immediate and typed: a path outside this workspace or
a temp dir, a file that is not really a PNG/JPEG, an oversize render,
or a per-run cap all come back as an error you can fix and retry now,
rather than a silent failure discovered after you are gone. Nothing
is ever downscaled or truncated behind your back — an image is either
stored exactly as you rendered it or refused.

**Do not put an attachment URL — or any other localhost link — in a
PR body.** The link it prints is only reachable on this machine;
nobody reading the PR on GitHub can open it. This applies whether or
not the evidence server is listening: if it isn't, the image is
still stored, but that is still not a reason to paste a URL or inline
it as base64. Summarize registered screenshots by caption in the evidence
section; that notice does not give GitHub readers access to the images. Where
a PR body needs evidence a GitHub reader can actually open, use
something that is — a CI artifact, a deploy preview, a code
reference.

## Probes

Messages may arrive between turns. Treat them as questions from a
human reviewer — short, specific answers.

## CI failure classification

Classify a CI failure by cause, then act — do not park, and do not leave "someone else will handle it" as an available excuse:
- Caused by your change: fix it. No judgement call, not grounds to block, no handoff.
- Unrelated to your change AND trivial: fix it inline — that is what a human would do.
- Unrelated AND not trivial: do not absorb it into this PR. Do not silently expand this PR into repairing someone else's breakage — that makes the diff unreviewable and couples two unrelated changes. This is the case where stopping to escalate can be legitimate. Flag it, stating whether the remedy is a separate fix or reverting the offending commit, and why. "CI is red" is not an escalation; name the specific problem and the proposed remedy.

Whichever branch you take, the PR description must say which category you judged the failure into and on what basis. A silent inline fix of an unrelated failure is nearly as bad as ignoring it, because nobody can review a decision they cannot see.

Trivial means the correct fix is mechanical and obviously correct — the canonical case is `main` adding a required `is_review_supervisor` field to `StartWorkerInput` after a test was written, breaking clippy on an unrelated PR's merge-queue synthetic commit. One line, mechanical. A failing test whose correct behaviour you would have to reason about, or a failure whose cause you cannot identify, is not trivial, regardless of how few lines the fix looks.

The engine already mints CI-fix revisions. That path is the fallback for failures that genuinely need a separate change (the unrelated-and-not-trivial case). It is not a reason to punt a failure you could fix in this run.


